  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nWe're monitoring convection mainly in the southern Coastal Bend \nearly this morning spinning around a broad mid-level disturbance in \nthe Bay of Campeche. A subtle convergence boundary will help to \nfocus most of the overnight convection to the immediate coast south \nof Port Aransas. With deep moisture still very high (2.46\" pwat at \n00Z), rainfall rates will continue to be very high within the \nstronger convective elements. With the boundary continuing to push \neastward, the most organized convection should also push offshore by \nmorning. Will still expect scattered showers and storms over South \nTexas during the morning and early afternoon tomorrow though, mainly \nsouth and east.  The boundary will continue to hug the coast over \nthe next few days and as a result, low rain chances will continue \nalong the immediate coast and especially offshore through at least \nMonday. \n\nBy Tuesday and Wednesday we're watching some drier mid-level air \napproach, but there's some question whether it makes it this far \nsouth or not. For now will remain on the side of it making it until \nand if there's more consistency in guidance saying otherwise. This \nwill bring a break from convection even to the coastal waters for a \ncouple of days before moisture comes back later in the week as \nanother mid-level low approaches Texas and gulf moisture pushes back \nin. Currently low to medium rain chances Thursday and Friday, \npossibly into Saturday.\n\nHigh temps remain in the 80s today and Monday, but the 90s start \ncreeping back in by Tuesday and through the middle of the week. Late \nweek should see at least a slight decrease in temps as the moisture \nincreases. \n\nThe steady northeasterly flow we will see through the first half of \nthe week will warrant keeping an eye on tide levels along the coast. \nThis is a flow that can often lead to wave run-up with Ekman \ntransport. Water levels on Saturday did not appear to make it far up \nthe beaches despite tidal values being fairly high, but suspect this \nwill change as this northeast flow becomes more persistent. Coastal \nflood advisories could be needed by early in the work week.",
